8.2  Race conditions, locks and atomics

TL;DR
  - A race: the result depends on which thread runs first. count += 1 is read,
    add, write: two threads can read the same value and lose one increment.
  - A lock (mutex) lets one thread at a time into a critical section. Atomics do
    one small operation indivisibly, with no lock.
  - Races hide: the code works a thousand times, then fails under load or on a
    machine with more cores.

      gcc -fsanitize=thread -g prog.c   # ThreadSanitizer finds data races
      go test -race ./...

On your machine
  CPUs      8 logical CPUs on 4 cores, up to 2 threads per core (SMT)

Pitfalls
  - Deadlock: thread A holds lock 1 and waits for 2, thread B holds 2 and waits
    for 1. Always take locks in the same order.
  - A lock protects data only if every access takes it, reads included.
  - "Check then act" (if the file doesn't exist, create it) races with other
    processes too: use atomic operations (O_EXCL, rename).
  - Holding a lock while waiting on the network or disk serialises everything
    behind it.

Deep dive

  ▾ Why can't threads just read and write shared variables?

    Compilers and CPUs reorder memory operations for speed, as long as one
    thread can't tell. Another thread can: it may see a flag set before the data
    it guards. Locks and atomics include barriers that forbid harmful
    reorderings, and make each core's cached copy coherent at the right time
    (2.3). Without them, even "harmless" races are undefined behaviour in C, C++
    and Rust.

  ▾ How do languages help?

    Rust refuses to compile data races: shared data must be behind a lock or an
    atomic. Go and Java define what racy code may observe, and ship race
    detectors. Message passing (channels, queues) avoids shared state
    altogether: one owner per piece of data, the others send it requests.

